<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=gregorian-calendar">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Writing and counting</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gregorian calendar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1582">1582</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as New Style calendar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gregorian calendar went into effect in October 1582 under the papal bull Inter gravissimas, issued on 24 February 1582 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-xiii">Pope Gregory XIII</a>, as a reform of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julian-calendar">Julian calendar</a>. It spaces leap years so that the average year is 365.2425 days, omitting the leap day in three centurial years every 400 years. The reform advanced the date by 10 days: Thursday 4 October 1582 was followed by Friday 15 October 1582. It modified a proposal by the Calabrian doctor Aloysius Lilius, and Christopher Clavius was its architect. Catholic countries adopted it first, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, the last European country, adopted it for civil use in 1923.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Gregory XIII introduced the <b>Gregorian calendar</b> in 1582, now used worldwide.</blockquote><a href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The calendar too was reformed, though the new <b>Gregorian calendar</b> came only in 1582.</blockquote><a href="/movement/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1582</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Papal States</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-xiii">Pope Gregory XIII</a> · Aloysius Lilius · Christopher Clavius · <a href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The Gregorian leap-year rule of 97 leap years in 400 years was put forward by Petrus Pitatus of Verona in 1560.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/julian-calendar">Julian calendar<span>46 BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=gregorian-calendar">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
